<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kollam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Quilon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kollam, formerly Quilon, is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was a major centre of the medieval spice trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, and the Malayalam calendar, the Kollam era, begins in 825 CE. The Tharisapalli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of 849 granted privileges to its Syrian Christian church, and it later became a seat of the rulers of Venad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kollam.jpg" alt="Kollam" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arunvrparavur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_India_Kollam_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…at the tip of the subcontinent had belonged to the Ay dynasty, the earliest rulers of southern Kerala, whose capital lay first at Aykudi and from the late 8th century at <b>Kollam</b>, where the Persian merchant Sulaiman al-Tajir found an extensive trade with China in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope John XXII, the second of the Avignon popes, created a Latin diocese at <b>Quilon</b>, in present-day Kollam in southern India, and appointed the French-born Jordanus as its first bishop.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-9">9 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Kodungallur and <b>Kollam</b> were major ports for the spice trade with Arabia and China.</blockquote><a href="/state/chera-perumals-of-makotai">Chera Perumals of Makotai</a></li><li><blockquote>Seeking to break the Portuguese hold on pepper, the Dutch East India Company (VOC) allied with the Zamorins of Calicut and took <b>Quilon</b> in 1661 and Cochin in 1663 from Portuguese India.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-malabar">Dutch Malabar</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tharisapalli copper plates of 849, in which the Venad chief Ayyanadikal Thiruvadikal granted land and privileges to the Syrian Christian church at <b>Kollam</b>, are among the earliest records of Kerala.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-venad">Kingdom of Venad</a></li></ul></section>
